.Dd September 19, 2026
.Dt GETPID 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm getpid ,
.Nm getppid ,
.Nm getpgid ,
.Nm getpgrp ,
.Nm setpgid ,
.Nm getsid ,
.Nm setsid
.Nd get or set process, process group, and session IDs
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In unistd.h
.Ft pid_t
.Fn getpid "void"
.Ft pid_t
.Fn getppid "void"
.Ft pid_t
.Fn getpgid "pid_t pid"
.Ft pid_t
.Fn getpgrp "void"
.Ft int
.Fn setpgid "pid_t pid" "pid_t pgid"
.Ft pid_t
.Fn getsid "pid_t pid"
.Ft pid_t
.Fn setsid "void"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn getpid
returns the calling process's ID.
.Fn getppid
returns its parent's ID.
.Fn getpgid
returns the process group ID of
.Fa pid ;
.Fn getsid
returns its session ID.
For either call, a
.Fa pid
of 0 selects the caller.
.Fn getpgrp
takes no arguments and returns the caller's process group ID.
.Pp
.Fn setpgid
sets the process group ID of
.Fa pid
to
.Fa pgid .
A
.Fa pid
of 0 selects the caller.
A
.Fa pgid
of 0 selects the target's own process ID, making it a group leader.
The target must be the caller or its child and must not be a zombie or
a session leader.
Unless the target is forming its own group, the destination group must
already have a member in the target's session.
The group's original leader need not still be alive.
.Pp
The kernel does not check whether a child has called
.Xr execve 2
or whether the child's session differs from the caller's session.
The destination group is checked against the target's session.
.Pp
.Fn setsid
creates a session and a process group, both with the caller's process ID.
The caller becomes the leader of both.
A caller that already leads a process group cannot create a session.
The console session and controlling terminal are described in
.Xr console 4 .
.Pp
The i386 system call numbers are 20 for
.Fn getpid ,
64 for
.Fn getppid ,
132 for
.Fn getpgid ,
65 for
.Fn getpgrp ,
57 for
.Fn setpgid ,
147 for
.Fn getsid ,
and 66 for
.Fn setsid .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The query functions return the requested ID.
.Fn setsid
returns the new session ID, which is the caller's process ID.
.Fn setpgid
returns 0 on success.
On failure, these functions return -1 and set
.Va errno .
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/jobprobe.c
forks a child, checks its inherited group and session, calls
.Fn setpgid "0" "0" ,
and checks that the child's process group ID equals its process ID.
A separate child calls
.Fn setsid
and checks that both its session ID and process group ID equal its process ID.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EINVAL]"
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fn setpgid
was given a negative
.Fa pgid .
.It Bq Er EPERM
The target of
.Fn setpgid
is a session leader, or the destination is neither its own process ID
nor an existing group in its session.
For
.Fn setsid ,
the caller already leads a process group.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
There is no current process, or the process requested by
.Fn getpgid ,
.Fn getsid ,
or
.Fn setpgid
does not exist.
For
.Fn setpgid ,
this error also means that the target is unused, is a zombie, or is
neither the caller nor its child.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr kill 2 ,
.Xr waitpid 2 ,
.Xr console 4
